# The Nodes list's sort row: what order the rows the filter kept are in, under the chips that
# decide which rows those are.
#
# Four things to watch, and the fourth is the one that would be a bug nobody could see.
#
#   - Two controls, two shapes. Three filters fit across the panel as chips at every glyph scale
#     and five orders do not, so the order is the shape Settings gives an enum this size: a label
#     saying which question it is, and the word it is set to. Two chip strips one above the other
#     would have been two questions with nothing on the frame naming either.
#   - The action bar names A per row. On the chips it reads "filter", on the row under them
#     "sort", and on a node "open" - the keycap whose verb changes is named per row.
#   - The heading's count does not move as the order steps. A filter changes how many rows there
#     are and a sort does not, which is the difference the two rows exist to keep apart.
#   - Under Distance the rows reorder. Under Distance with no fix of our own they cannot, and the
#     value column says so rather than leaving a list that did not move under a word that says it
#     did - the filter chips' failure one axis over.

scene demo
scale 4
clock 2026-01-12 19:12
config

pin ECHO

tab nodes
hold 1600

# Onto the sort row. The chips above stay on All, so everything that moves below is the order.
key down
hold 1400

# Default -> Recent. The client's own ranking floats the pinned node and the people you talk to;
# this is the plain question the phone apps open on, and the first row under the lead rows moves.
key a
hold 2200

# Recent -> Name. The answer to the search this client is not going to have: a name you would
# know on sight, at a place you can scroll to, with no keyboard to type it on.
key a
hold 2200

# Name -> Distance. The one the chips genuinely cannot answer - "near me" is an ordering of all
# of them rather than a kind of node - and the reason this row exists at all.
key a
hold 2400

# The rows under it are still rows. The cursor walks off both controls into them and A opens the
# node the row drew, which is the half that would look right while being wrong.
key down 3
hold 1200
key a
hold 1800
key b
hold 700

# Distance -> Hops, the same question in mesh terms, which still works when nobody is sharing a
# position at all.
key up 3
key a
hold 2200

# And round to the order the list has always been in, which is where a reader who never touches
# this row stays.
key a
hold 2000
